# The `permissions` API # {#permissions-api}

The <dfn id="permissions-api-concept">permissions</dfn> API lets an extension check, request, and relinquish its own
[=optional permissions=] and [=optional host permissions=] at runtime, instead of requiring
every permission at install time.

Issue: [=Optional permissions=] and [=optional host permissions=] are not yet defined (see the
empty [`optional_permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-optional_permissions) and
[`optional_host_permissions`](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#key-optional_host_permissions) sections). Until defined, take them to mean: a
permission or host pattern that is either listed in `optional_permissions`/`optional_host_permissions`,
or listed in `permissions`/`host_permissions` and withheld from the extension until requested.
This relationship is under active WECG discussion, with two competing restructurings still
unsettled as of 2026-07-02: [w3c/webextensions#798](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/pull/798)
and [#883](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/883).

## Types ## {#permissions-types}

### The `Permissions` dictionary ### {#permissions-dictionary}

A {{Permissions}} dictionary's `permissions` member is a list of named API permission strings,
excluding host permissions. Its `origins` member is a list of [=match pattern|match patterns=],
validated using the same [=match pattern=] grammar used for the `host_permissions` manifest key.

Which strings are valid entries in `permissions` is implementation-defined: each browser
recognizes its own set of permission names, and a name outside a given browser's set is not a
valid entry for that browser.

Note: As of 2026-09, sixteen permission names are recognized by Chrome, Firefox, and Safari
alike: `activeTab`, `alarms`, `clipboardWrite`, `contextMenus`, `cookies`,
`declarativeNetRequest`, `declarativeNetRequestFeedback`, `declarativeNetRequestWithHostAccess`,
`nativeMessaging`, `notifications`, `scripting`, `storage`, `tabs`, `unlimitedStorage`,
`webNavigation`, `webRequest`. A name outside this list may be recognized by one or two of the
three but must not be assumed portable.

<div class=issue>
An entry that is well formed but is not a name the browser recognizes
fails differently in each:

* Chrome: rejects the returned promise.
* Firefox: throws synchronously.
* Safari: throws synchronously for `contains()`, but rejects the promise
    for `request()` and `remove()`.

</div>

<pre class="idl">
dictionary Permissions {
  sequence&lt;DOMString&gt; permissions;
  sequence&lt;DOMString&gt; origins;
};
</pre>

## Methods ## {#permissions-methods}

Issue: Chrome, Firefox, and Safari additionally accept a callback function as a trailing
argument on every method here, resolved/invoked instead of returning a promise when supplied.
This specification defines only the promise form here; see [Promises and callbacks](https://w3c.github.io/webextensions/specification/#promises-and-callbacks) (currently empty)
for how dual promise/callback signatures are expressed generally.

<pre class="idl">
[Exposed=WebExtensions]
interface permissions {
  Promise&lt;Permissions&gt; getAll();
  Promise&lt;boolean&gt; contains(optional Permissions permissions);
  Promise&lt;boolean&gt; request(optional Permissions permissions);
  Promise&lt;undefined&gt; remove(optional Permissions permissions);

  readonly attribute PermissionsEvent onAdded;
  readonly attribute PermissionsEvent onRemoved;
};
</pre>

### `getAll()` ### {#permissions-getall}

<dfn method for="permissions">getAll()</dfn> returns the extension's complete current set of
permissions and [=match pattern|match patterns=]: both the permissions and host permissions
required by the manifest, and any optional or runtime-host permissions currently granted.

1. Let |result| be a new {{Permissions}} dictionary.
1. Set |result|["permissions"] to the extension's currently active named API permissions,
    combining permissions required by the manifest and optional permissions currently granted.
1. Set |result|["origins"] to the extension's currently active host
    [=match pattern|match patterns=], combining host permissions required by the manifest and
    optional or runtime host permissions currently granted.
1. Return |result|.

Issue: May the returned `origins` list include a synthesized "matches all hosts" pattern that is
not literally present in the manifest or in any single granted permission, when the union of
currently granted host access happens to cover all hosts? Safari does this; Chrome and Firefox
return only the origins actually declared or granted.

### `contains()` ### {#permissions-contains}

<dfn method for="permissions">contains(permissions)</dfn> checks whether the extension currently
has every permission and origin named in |permissions|.

1. Let |permissions| be the method's argument.
1. For each named permission in |permissions|["permissions"]: if it is not currently active on
    the extension, return `false`.
1. For each [=match pattern=] in |permissions|["origins"]: if it is not subsumed by a currently
    active host [=match pattern=] on the extension, return `false`.
1. Return `true`.

### `request()` ### {#permissions-request}

<dfn method for="permissions">request(permissions)</dfn> asks the user to grant the extension
some or all of |permissions|, where those are not already active. It must only be called while
handling a [=user activation=].

1. Let |permissions| be the method's argument.
1. If this call is not happening within a [=user activation=], reject with an error.
1. If any named permission in |permissions|["permissions"] is not declared in the extension's
    `optional_permissions` manifest key, reject with an error.
1. If any [=match pattern=] in |permissions|["origins"] is not subsumed by a [=match pattern=]
    declared in the extension's `optional_host_permissions` manifest key, or by a
    `host_permissions` entry withheld from the extension pending user approval, reject with an
    error.
1. If every named permission and [=match pattern=] in |permissions| is already active on the
    extension, resolve with `true`.
1. Prompt the user to approve the permissions and origins in |permissions| that are not already
    active. User agents may skip the prompt, and grant automatically, when the newly requested
    permissions and origins carry no additional user-facing warnings beyond what the extension
    already has active.
1. If the user does not approve the prompt, resolve with `false`.
1. Grant the extension the requested permissions and origins that were not already active. This
    fires {{permissions/onAdded}} for the newly granted set.
1. Resolve with `true`.

<div class=issue>
The browsers differ on what happens when no window is available to show
the prompt in, separately from the user activation requirement:

* Chrome: rejects, distinctly from the no-activation rejection.
* Firefox: no window requirement. Prompts through whatever surface is available.
* Safari: no window concept. The embedding application prompts, and the
    call resolves false if it never responds.

</div>

Chrome and Safari exclude the entire `permissions` namespace from content scripts. Firefox
exposes `request()` alone to content scripts, keeping the rest of the namespace restricted to
background and extension-page contexts.

WebKit's extension engine grants no permission on its own; `request()` always defers to the
embedding application through the `WKWebExtensionControllerDelegate` prompt callbacks. WebKit
also exposes `deniedPermissions`/`deniedPermissionMatchPatterns` as properties the application
sets directly, and a permission or host present there is excluded from the request before the
prompt callback is ever invoked, so `request()` resolves without showing UI for it. Safari uses
exactly this mechanism for host permissions on supervised, MDM-managed devices: the
`com.apple.configuration.safari.extensions.settings` payload's `AllowedDomains`/`DeniedDomains`
keys pre-decide per-extension host access, with a denied domain always taking precedence. So a
managed policy can and does cause Safari's `request()` to resolve without a prompt for a denied
host, the same behavioral effect as Chrome's and Firefox's enterprise-blocked path
([w3c/webextensions#260](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/260), open as of
2026-05-04), implemented at the application layer rather than as an engine-level policy check.

Issue: Apple's documented MDM schema for Safari extensions covers host/domain access only
(`AllowedDomains`/`DeniedDomains`); it defines no equivalent key for non-host API permissions
such as `tabs` or `downloads`. WebKit's engine exposes the same pre-denial primitive for those
permissions (`WKWebExtensionContext.deniedPermissions`), but whether Safari populates it from
policy is not documented anywhere public, and Safari's application source is closed.

### `remove()` ### {#permissions-remove}

<dfn method for="permissions">remove(permissions)</dfn> relinquishes some or all of the
extension's currently active optional permissions and origins.

1. Let |permissions| be the method's argument.
1. If any named permission or [=match pattern=] in |permissions| is required by the extension's
    manifest (not optional, and not a withheld host permission the extension chose to give up
    separately), reject with an error.
1. If any named permission or [=match pattern=] in |permissions| is not declared anywhere in the
    extension's manifest, reject with an error.
1. Revoke each named permission and [=match pattern=] in |permissions| that is currently active
    on the extension. Entries in |permissions| that are not currently active are ignored rather
    than treated as an error. This fires {{permissions/onRemoved}} for the revoked set, if any.
1. Resolve with `undefined`.

<div class=issue>
Why does `remove()` resolve with `undefined` here, when every shipping browser resolves `true`?

* All three browsers currently resolve `true` on success.
* [w3c/webextensions#1069](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/1069) (open) argues the
    boolean is meaningless, since `remove()` never resolves `false`.
* The sibling case is `alarms.clearAll()`,
    [w3c/webextensions#1055](https://github.com/w3c/webextensions/issues/1055); the 2026-08-13
    WECG minutes record a Mozilla engineer saying he would be fine dropping its return value too.

This specification follows #1069 rather than current behavior; no browser resolves `undefined`
here today. `request()` and `contains()` are not affected: both resolve a boolean that carries
real information (whether the request was granted, whether the extension has the permission),
so neither changes.
</div>

## Events ## {#permissions-events}

Issue: {{PermissionsEvent}} below is a minimal local stand-in for the WebExtensions
event-listener pattern (`addListener()`, `removeListener()`, `hasListener()`) used by every
event in this specification. A shared `events.Event` definition belongs in a common section of
the specification, not duplicated in each namespace that fires one.

<pre class="idl">
callback PermissionsListener = undefined (optional Permissions changed);

interface PermissionsEvent {
  undefined addListener(PermissionsListener listener);
  undefined removeListener(PermissionsListener listener);
  boolean hasListener(PermissionsListener listener);
};
</pre>

### `onAdded` ### {#permissions-onadded}

The <dfn attribute for="permissions">onAdded</dfn> event fires with a {{Permissions}}
dictionary listing the permissions and origins newly granted to the extension, whenever the
extension's active permissions increase. This includes grants made through
{{permissions/request}}, and grants made through user agent UI outside of the `permissions` API
(for example, a browser action letting the user grant a withheld host permission).

Chrome explicitly does not fire `onAdded`/`onRemoved` for permission or host-permission grants
driven entirely by administrative or managed policy. Whether Firefox's and Safari's
policy-driven grants go through the same code path as user- and API-driven grants, and therefore
also fire the event, is unconfirmed.

### `onRemoved` ### {#permissions-onremoved}

The <dfn attribute for="permissions">onRemoved</dfn> event fires with a {{Permissions}}
dictionary listing the permissions and origins no longer granted to the extension, whenever the
extension's active permissions decrease, whether through {{permissions/remove}} or through user
agent UI.
